A||||2||Gold-86SE||Clutch problems on an '84||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||06-20-2000||09:29 PM||||<BR>Ok guys, here's the deal.<P>had problems with my wifes 84 2m4 not shifting into gears, and if by chance you happened to get the thing into gear, it would not pull out of gear, so here is what i did.<P>replaced the alum. pedal with a metal pedal from an 87.  noticed some paint corrosion from in the location of the master cylinder, so it was replaced with a mc from another 84.  and bled the system acording to v8 archies notes.<P>now the result.<BR>still does not shift into gears and the pedal feels extremely soft, like there is nothing that i am pushing against.<P>what is my problem(s)?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||06-20-2000||10:11 PM||||<BR>found my problem, misunderstood archies instructions, after re-reading i did exactly what he said but that did not work either, so here is my solution.<P>use his instructions to the t, except add one item to step 10.<P>He said "10) (You) Top off fluid in reservoir"<P>I say "10) (You) Top off fluid in reservoir, AND ALSO REPLACE THE RESERVOIR CAP."<P>if you do this, it will work, will maintain proper pressure while bleeding the clutch system.<P>chalk it up to my inexperience, since this was my first clutch bleed, but this worked for me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||06-20-2000||10:14 PM||||It's possible you may still have some air in the system.  These things can be a real pain to bleed!  You may also want to check the release lever at the transaxle that the slave cylinder pushrod pushes against.  The early Fieros had a release lever that was made from stamped sheet metal. It is prone to cracking and flexing over a period of time.  Pontiac replaced it with one made from a solid piece that seems to last forever.||||reg||3
